Q5 Explanation — VACUUM REINDEX vs ANALYZE

This is a critical distinction for interleaved sort keys:
Table

Operation


What It Does


When to Use
VACUUM SORT ONLY	Re-sorts rows in compound sort key order	After loads on compound sort key tables
VACUUM DELETE ONLY	Reclaims space from deleted rows	After many DELETEs
VACUUM REINDEX	Rebuilds the interleaved sort key index	✅ After large loads on INTERLEAVED sort key tables
ANALYZE	Updates table statistics for query planner	After loads (helps optimizer, NOT sort order)

KEY RULE:
├── COMPOUND sort key + new data loaded → VACUUM SORT ONLY
├── INTERLEAVED sort key + new data loaded → VACUUM REINDEX
├── Many deletes → VACUUM DELETE ONLY
├── ANALYZE → updates statistics only (doesn't fix sort order!)
└── ANALYZE helps query PLANNING, not query EXECUTION on unsorted data

Why ANALYZE is wrong here:

    The question says "query performance degrades after loading 100M rows"
    This is a SORT problem (data is unsorted), not a statistics problem
    ANALYZE updates statistics but doesn't re-sort or rebuild the index
    Interleaved sort keys specifically need REINDEX (not just SORT) because the interleaving zones must be recalculated

    💡 Memorize: Interleaved sort key + large load = VACUUM REINDEX (higher maintenance cost — this is the trade-off of interleaved vs compound)

